WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:31.532
I am Harold Varmus. I am a cancer biologist,

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:35.266
and I am the president of Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center.

00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:39.599
I'd like to talk to you a little bit about how somebody becomes a scientist.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.166
Many people think that scientists are born.

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:45.499
They are born with special properties.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.299
They listen to Mozart in their crib. They excel at math even in grade school.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.166
They join the chess club.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:54.699
They don't do athletics.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.799
They end up being victors in science fairs before they get to college,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.432
and they enter a track that leads to advanced degrees in science and engineering.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:12.199
Of course, this sometimes happens and reinforces the popular image,

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.899
but there are lots of ways to become a scientist.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:20.066
And it is important at a time when the US is losing numbers

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:26.166
of scientists and not training enough to sustain our leadership in industry

00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:32.532
and the sciences to think about other ways in which people do become scientists.

00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:37.899
I had, myself, an unusual route to being a scientist,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.732
and I'd like to spend a little bit of time telling you about what that route was like.

00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:45.432
So I was a good, but not exceptional student,

00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:49.399
in public high school in Freeport, NY on Long Island.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.199
I didn't have strong science teachers.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.532
I didn't show any particular interest in science.

00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:57.366
I didn't spend my afternoons with a chemistry set.

00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.299
I spent more of them playing tennis and going to the beach

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:06.466
or reading novels, than I did attempting to win science fairs.

00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:10.566
My father was a doctor, and I expected to follow him into medicine.

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:15.299
And after high school, I went off to Amherst College as a pre-medical student.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.199
And there, at Amherst, placed before me was a very rich table

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.632
of intellectual opportunities in physics,

00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:26.532
and philosophy, literature and history,

00:02:26.533 --> 00:02:32.966
and I found myself spending most of my time running the college newspaper

00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:39.532
and doing a thesis on Charles Dickens and spending time with my literary friends.

00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:43.566
I continued to think about medical school,

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:46.032
but didn't do particularly well in Chemistry and decided

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:49.599
to finally go off to graduate school in English literature,

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.499
where I spent a year before deciding again to think about medical school.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.166
After being rejected by one, Harvard,

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.932
I chose to go to another, Columbia.

00:03:01.933 --> 00:03:07.766
And I found myself engrossed in sequence in

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:11.299
psychiatry and then internal medicine...

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:16.399
never paying much attention to serious laboratory research,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.932
but interested in other serious forms of research in the clinic.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:23.399
But not really engaged in any of those forms.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.732
I did house staff training, and then at the age of 28,

00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:31.799
the Vietnam War required that I make a decision

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:38.132
about a way to serve my country without serving a war I didn't believe in.

00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:41.066
I was able to do that thanks to a program that allowed

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.866
those of us who were dissenters from the war

00:03:43.867 --> 00:03:46.499
to work at the National Institutes of Health.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.232
And here, for reasons beyond this discussion,

00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:57.466
I was plunged into work on the molecular biology of E. coli;

00:03:57.467 --> 00:04:00.866
the way in which bacteria regulate the expression

00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:03.366
of a set of genes called the lac operon.

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:08.032
This exposed me for the first time to the excitement of laboratory research.

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:11.499
I was a lot older than most students who write to me

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:17.132
today to work on an Intel project in my laboratory at Sloan Kettering,

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:22.032
but, nevertheless, I still had the same kind of enthusiasm

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:25.499
I see in those high school students for understanding

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.266
how a bacterial organism controls its genes.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:33.299
Molecular biology was in its early phases

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.232
and the idea of using bacteria or other simple organisms

00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:40.732
as model organisms for understanding

00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:42.899
higher levels of regulation

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.332
in complex organisms was still, again, in its early stages.

00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:50.532
And this was an exhilerating time, but it was not something

00:04:50.533 --> 00:04:53.732
I had been trained for, and I wanted to get back

00:04:53.733 --> 00:04:56.699
to something more medically related, which I did,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.099
when I discovered through coursework at the NIH that the...

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.466
that there was new excitement about viruses:

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:07.999
very small packets of genes that were able

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.766
to turn a normal cell into a cancer cell.

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:13.166
Cancer was an important, and is an important,

00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:16.066
human disease. I was interested in trying to understand

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:18.166
with the new tools of molecular biology.

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:22.132
At that point, at the age of 30, I came to the University of California,

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:26.799
San Francisco to begin what proved to be over 2 decades of work

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:32.432
on how cancer viruses grow, and how they turn a normal cell into a cancer cell.

00:05:32.433 --> 00:05:35.332
The reason I tell this story

00:05:35.333 --> 00:05:39.299
is to illustrate that there are lots of ways to become a scientist,

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:42.666
and that in the US one of the great advantages

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:48.666
we have is that we don't require that people make career decisions at an early age.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:51.932
We allow, we even encourage what I would call

00:05:51.933 --> 00:05:54.832
a prolonged adolescence.  This is something to keep

00:05:54.833 --> 00:05:58.866
in mind as you progress through high school and college.

00:05:58.867 --> 00:06:01.132
There is still time to make decisions

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:05.232
about the way you are going to conduct your careers.

00:06:05.233 --> 00:06:07.332
Moreover, it is important to remember

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:12.632
that not every scientist is a math whiz as a grade school student.

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:15.432
There are lots of ways to become a scientist,

00:06:15.433 --> 00:06:17.832
and lots of ways to be a scientist.

00:06:17.833 --> 00:06:22.966
In fact, I would argue that while science can involve

00:06:22.967 --> 00:06:27.666
experimental procedures and analysis of classical experiments,

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:30.632
there are lots of other ways to think like a scientist.

00:06:30.633 --> 00:06:33.966
Even in fields that are outside of what we call

00:06:33.967 --> 00:06:36.499
the conventional natural sciences.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.766
So thinking like a scientist means thinking about evidence,

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:44.499
gathering it, evaluating it, and interpreting it

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:48.332
in a way that helps to make sense of the world in which we live.

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:51.399
We need more of this in our world

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.999
and it is with an effort to understand

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.532
that there are lots of paths to developing a scientific outlook

00:06:59.533 --> 00:07:03.366
that can influence your career and help strengthen our society

00:07:03.367 --> 00:07:08.132
that I offer this brief story of an unconventional way

00:07:08.133 --> 01:00:00.000
to become a scientist.

